A||||11||fiero56||fresh 2.8; magnet in oil pan?||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||09-27-2001||08:30 PM||||The project is on hold at the moment(awaiting payment on bad checks I received), but figure it would be a good idea to slap a magnet in the oil pan before final assembly. <P>Any concerns with this? My main worry is the magnet collecting shavings, and dropping them off as time goes on only to let them circulate, and recirculate after they are caught again, etc. Any problems with 2 magnets a few inches apart? Should I skip teh magnets and let the filter do its thing?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||09-27-2001||08:45 PM||||Seeing as how removing the pan is a PITA job, I would leave the magnets out and let the filter 'do its thing'.  <P>It really wouldn't hurt anything if you did, because any particles that fall off the magnet will go through the filter, but since you can't readily take the magnet out and clean it off/inspect it I wouldn't bother.<P>Although, it would be interesting to see how much material collects on it in say a few years or whenever you pull the pan off again.<P>I guess I'm flip-flopping here, so do it or not......it's your choice.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodney||09-27-2001||09:07 PM||||I have magnet oil pan plugs on my web site now. On my old S-10 pick up truck I put some on the outside of the steel oil pan figuring it was better than nothing and I got them free from where I worked. My new S-10 has an aluminum pan but came with a factory magnetic oil plug.<P>Rodney Dickman <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||09-28-2001||08:17 AM||||I would have no problem putting a magnet in my oil pan.  In fact, on my rebuild I was going to, thought I shouldn't, left it out and then put one on the outside when it was installed.  Later thinkings left me wishing I would have put it inside the pan.<P>A magnet will keep its magnetism if it is actually attracted to something (this keeps the magnetic field strong) and will start to dissipate it's magnetic field when it is not attracted to anything for long lengths of time ... at least that's what I remember from physics, but I've slept since then.  So, I see no reason why a magnet would be giving up shavings unless it is just plain full of them which would be cause in itself to be dropping the pan.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||09-28-2001||08:40 AM||||I considered this when I dropped my oil pan to install the return oil line for the turbo. I'm sure it would not be a problem but I felt I did not want to risk the magnet moving around and possibly causing some damage. Although I feel that would not happen I did not want to risk it. If you do put one in the pan silicone it down. That should help. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||09-28-2001||03:31 PM||||I personally wouldn't put a magnet in the oil pan, but you can get magnets to drop into the oil filter from Jegs, and other places.  That way you get the magnet's benefits, but you can change/clean the magnet at each oil change.||||reg||13
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||09-28-2001||03:36 PM||||Could you just attache a magnetic band around the outside of the filter.  As long as the magnets were strong enough it would have the same effect.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||09-28-2001||03:47 PM||||Yes you could put a magnet on the filter, but I wouldn't count on it staying on just due the surface area that the magnet would have to attract to the filter, of course you get a super strong magnet, but then why wouldn't you put it on the bottom of the pan???<P>Anyway, putting a magnetic in the pan itself won't hurt anything at all.  I don't really see where silicon would benefit anything unless you cleaned the oil pan thoroughly so you could get the silicon to adhere.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mickey_Moose||09-28-2001||06:14 PM||||why would you need a magnet around the filter?!?? The filter would take any particles out (if it doesn't then, I would suggest getting a better filter.<P>Magnets in the oil pan &gt;&gt; well you would have to pull the pan to clean the magnet, and what's going to prevent it from say 'bouncing' up and hitting the crank?<P>best &gt;&gt; use a magnetic plug, stays put, and you can clean it each time you dump the oil without having to remove the pan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>just my thoughts...<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000009||fiero56||09-28-2001||07:26 PM||||Inital start-up is what worries me most, those first 20 minutes with all the shavings floating around. <P>Oil pan is steel. If I hit something hard enough to break the magnet loose and rise 6-8 inches to crank-shaft area, I've got bigger worries than hitting the crank. Inside of oil pan has a "shelf" mid-way up also. I don't see a drain plug catching a whole lot. I figure one large round strong magnet would work fine. Some A/Ts have magnets in the pan, but haven't heard of many people using them in engines.<P>BTW: It took me several months, but I finally found an engine brush set. FINALLY!<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-28-2001||07:41 PM||||Magnets in the pan will not move as long as you pick them with a few things in mind. All of you with automatics already have a rather large magnet in that.... The only way that one will ever move maybe is durring a wreck.<P>There are a couple issues with on in the oil pan... <P>you have to put it where it won't hold oil away from the drain plug. You also don't want a hole in it that will retain oil. (the tranny one is a donut but that whole pan comes off at oil change time.)<P>You want it big enough to collect lots of crap and hold it basically forever. This means most magnets you are used to won't do. those sheet magnets used for refrigerator decorations won't work. Useing several small "ceramic" magnets might be decent.<P>Keep in mind, the oil pump is before the filter. The filter will trap whatever before it reaches the engine but the pump is pretty naked.<P>I run a magnetic oil plug since I haven't had the pan off. It's always got at least some stuff on it.<P>Is a bigger magnet in the oil pan really needed? Probably not. It's not just an issue of cleaning the magnet. Its just that if the engine is shedding that much metal then there's probably a major problem.<P>The main thing after a rebuild is a short oil change cycle... 500-1000 miles... This first oil will wash out any remaining crap from the rebuild. No matter how well it's cleaned at the shop there's always something left for the oil to find... I'd put in a mag drain plug but that's probably about it.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jefrysuko||09-29-2001||12:26 AM||||The problem I see with having magnets inside the pan is that they will keep any metal shavings inside there also.  It is my opinion that if my motor starts doing something to produce shavings I will want to know about it.  I have started filtering my used oil through cheese cloth and am looking for a way to cut open the filter to check it also.<P>I figure as far as magnets go I am going to keep them outside the pan and then take them off before I drain the oil so that any shavings come out with the oil for me to see.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
